<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rio Grande do Sul</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> do Sul is the southernmost province, now a state, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> it was the scene of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ragamuffin-war">Farroupilha</a> or Ragamuffin War of 1835–1845, in which republican separatists maintained an independent republic for nearly a decade. Accounts of the Brazilian Empire cite the conflict as one of the regional rebellions that tested the young empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-sul.jpg" alt="Rio Grande do Sul" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Srfortes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandeira_do_Rio_Grande_do_Sul.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> do Sul, meaning Great River of the South, lies in the southernmost part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. It is bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-catarina">Santa Catarina</a> to the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> to the south and southwest, and the Argentine provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corrientes">Corrientes</a> and Misiones to the west. Its climate is predominantly humid subtropical, and in winter a cold southwesterly wind called the minuano sometimes lowers the temperature below freezing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the arrival of Portuguese and Spanish settlers the area was inhabited mostly by the Guarani and Kaingang peoples. The first Europeans there were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, followed by settlers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a>. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> the region was to be Spanish, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s interest lay on the Pacific coast and the River Plate, and the Spanish introduced livestock that escaped into the plains and attracted gauchos. The colonial province saw small wars and border skirmishes between Portugal and Spain. The Portuguese Crown distributed land in enormous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latifundium">latifundia</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raising predominated, while Azorean settlers on smaller properties introduced wheat, the main export until the early 19th century.</p><p>In the 19th century the state was the scene of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ragamuffin-war">Ragamuffin War</a>, wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguayan-war">Paraguayan War</a>. In the war against the Argentine dictator Juan Manuel Rosas, 75 percent of the Brazilian troops were gaúchos. In 1865 a Paraguayan division occupied Uruguaiana, and troops of the Triple Alliance besieged it until the Paraguayans surrendered. In 1930 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a>, then state president, led a revolt that overthrew the federal government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> do Sul is a state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> divided into 497 municipalities, with Porto Alegre as its capital and largest city. It shares a gaucho culture with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>, and its people are known for drinking chimarrão, a local version of mate, and for eating churrasco. Large waves of German and Italian migration have shaped the state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rio Grande do Sul” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Regency period (1831–1840) saw serial provincial revolts, including the Cabanagem in the Amazon (1835–1840), which killed an estimated 30,000 to 40,000 people, and the Farroupilha or Ragamuffin War in <b>Rio Grande do Sul</b> (1835–1845), in which separatists maintained an independent republic for nearly a decade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the unstable regency period in the Empire of Brazil, discontent in Salvador over central control and military recruitment for the war in <b>Rio Grande do Sul</b> came to a head.</blockquote><a href="/state/bahia-republic">Bahia Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>After the 1930 election, Getúlio Vargas of <b>Rio Grande do Sul</b> led the Revolution of 1930, which deposed President Washington Luís and opened the Second Brazilian Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-brazilian-republic">First Brazilian Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The rebels withdrew to <b>Rio Grande do Sul</b>, and the republic ended after about four months.</blockquote><a href="/state/juliana-republic">Juliana Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>In September 1835 ranchers of <b>Rio Grande do Sul</b>, angry at imperial taxes that favoured imported jerked beef, rose against the Empire of Brazil under Bento Gonçalves in the Ragamuffin War, or Farroupilha.</blockquote><a href="/state/riograndense-republic">Riograndense Republic</a></li></ul></section>
